---
import ProjectLayout from '../../../components/ProjectLayout.astro';
import BubbleGrid from '../../../components/BubbleGrid.astro';
import Bubble from '../../../components/Bubble.astro';
import MetricGrid from '../../../components/MetricGrid.astro';
import Metric from '../../../components/Metric.astro';

const pageTitle = 'Python Random Scripts';
const pageDescription = 'A living collection of small Python utilities where I explore everyday automation, quick data experiments, and CLI helpers. Each script solves a specific problem and doubles as a playground for sharpening language features and third-party packages.';
const repoUrl = 'https://github.com/lefos13/python-random-scripts';
---

<ProjectLayout 
	title={pageTitle} 
	description={pageDescription} 
	repoUrl={repoUrl}
	theme="purple"
>
	<BubbleGrid ariaLabel="Script categories" theme="purple">
		<Bubble title="Automation">
			From renaming media libraries to posting daily digests, these jobs showcase how cron-friendly
			Python scripts tame repetitive work.
		</Bubble>
		<Bubble title="Data Experiments">
			Lightweight notebooks and CLI parsers that clean CSV batches, reshape JSON, and visualize quick
			stats with pandas and Rich.
		</Bubble>
		<Bubble title="Developer Tools">
			Terminal helpers, template generators, and git-aware utilities that improve day-to-day workflows
			for collaborating teams.
		</Bubble>
	</BubbleGrid>

	<h2>What Lives in the Repo</h2>
	<p>
		The repository is intentionally eclectic: batch file renamers, API data fetchers, CSV cleaners, and
		shell-friendly tooling. It is where ideas start lightweight before they grow into standalone
		services or libraries.
	</p>

	<MetricGrid theme="purple">
		<Metric value="10+" label="Scripts maintained" />
		<Metric value="5 min" label="Average setup time" />
		<Metric value="12" label="Libraries explored" />
	</MetricGrid>

	<h3 id="highlights">Highlights</h3>
	<ul>
		<li>Hands-on automation scripts for file management, notifications, and workflow shortcuts.</li>
		<li>Data wrangling helpers that tidy CSV and JSON payloads with pandas and native tooling.</li>
		<li>Command-line experiments that showcase argparse patterns, logging, and packaging basics.</li>
	</ul>

	<h3>Why It Matters</h3>
	<p>
		Keeping a sandbox like this lets me iterate quickly, try new libraries, and carry working patterns
		into production projects. It also gives collaborators tangible, copy-ready snippets for their own
		use cases.
	</p>

	<p class="cta">
		Browse the scripts, star the repository, or open an issue with your own automation idea:
		<a href={repoUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{repoUrl}</a>.
	</p>
</ProjectLayout>
